---
import BaseHead from "../components/BaseHead.astro";
import Header from "../components/Header.astro";
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";
import UniqDemo from "../components/UniqDemo.astro";
import { SITE_TITLE, UNIQ_BASE } from "../consts";

const claim = `# the key is one you already have: the delivery id
curl -fsS -X POST "${UNIQ_BASE}/v1/uniq/$DELIVERY_ID?ttl=86400" \\
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $NANO_API_KEY"
# 201 {"first_time": true,  "hits": 1}   <- yours, do the work
# 200 {"first_time": false, "hits": 2}   <- somebody already did`;

const shell = `RESPONSE=$(curl -sf -X POST "${UNIQ_BASE}/v1/uniq/$DELIVERY_ID" \\
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $NANO_API_KEY")

if [ "$(jq -r .first_time <<< "$RESPONSE")" = "true" ]; then
  ./process.sh "$DELIVERY_ID" || curl -sf -X DELETE \\
    "${UNIQ_BASE}/v1/uniq/$DELIVERY_ID" -H "Authorization: Bearer $NANO_API_KEY"
fi`;
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
	<head>
		<BaseHead
			title={`NanoUniq — ${SITE_TITLE}`}
			description="Has this key arrived before? You supply the key, it supplies the memory. Duplicate webhook deliveries, without a table and a unique index. Free."
		/>
	</head>
	<body>
		<Header />
		<main>
			<section class="hero">
				<div>
					<p class="eyebrow">nano-api / uniq</p>
					<h1>The same webhook,<br /><span class="dim">delivered three times</span></h1>
					<p class="lede">
						Almost every queue and webhook is at-least-once, so the duplicate is not a bug you
						can fix upstream. You hand this a key you already have and it tells you whether that
						key has arrived before.
					</p>
					<p class="cta">
						<a class="button" href="/howto/#setup">Take a key</a>
						<a class="button secondary" href="/howto/#uniq">Curl walkthrough</a>
					</p>
					<p class="fineprint muted">
						Free. Signup is one <code>curl</code> and the key comes back in the response. No form, no
						card, nobody to ask.
					</p>
				</div>
				<UniqDemo />
			</section>

			<section class="band">
				<h2>It does not make the key. You already have one.</h2>
				<p>
					A Stripe event id, a delivery id, an order number, a message id. Generating a unique
					value is a pure function and your language already has it. What it cannot give you is
					memory that every instance of your app shares and that survives a deploy.
				</p>
				<pre><code>{claim}</code></pre>
				<p class="muted small">
					The status code carries the answer as well as the body: <code>201</code> the first time,
					<code>200</code> after.
				</p>
			</section>

			<section class="band">
				<h2>What goes wrong without one</h2>
				<div class="two">
					<div class="card">
						<p class="tag">A table and a unique index</p>
						<ul class="plain">
							<li>Two deliveries land together, both <code>SELECT</code>, both see nothing</li>
							<li>Both proceed, and the card is charged twice</li>
							<li>The fix is to let the insert be the check, which is not what you wrote</li>
							<li>And now you own a table that grows forever</li>
						</ul>
					</div>
					<div class="card alive-edge">
						<p class="tag live">With NanoUniq</p>
						<ul class="plain">
							<li>One statement decides; the insert <em>is</em> the check</li>
							<li>Forty simultaneous claims produce exactly one winner</li>
							<li>Rows expire on their own, so nothing grows forever</li>
							<li>No migration, no index, no cleanup job</li>
						</ul>
					</div>
				</div>
			</section>

			<section class="band">
				<h2>Claim, work, and give it back if the work failed</h2>
				<pre><code>{shell}</code></pre>
				<p class="muted small">
					Without the <code>DELETE</code>, a crash between claiming and finishing means the work
					never happens at all — the key is held and every retry is told it is late. That is the
					one sharp edge here, and it is the same trade every idempotency layer makes.
				</p>
			</section>

			<section class="band">
				<h2>What the guarantee is — and is not</h2>
				<p>
					<strong>Exactly one caller is told <code>first_time: true</code>.</strong> That is decided
					by the hit count the write returns, not by comparing timestamps: two arrivals in the same
					second would both match "first seen now", and both would be told they were first, which is
					the one answer this must never give twice.
				</p>
				<p>
					<strong>A key claims a window, not eternity.</strong> Default 24 hours, 30 days at most,
					then it expires and the next arrival is new again. Set the ttl above your upstream's
					retry horizon. Permanent memory would make this a database rather than an operation, and
					the whole point is that you do not have to run one.
				</p>
				<p class="muted small">
					Looking is free and changes nothing: <code>GET /v1/uniq/:key</code> works with a
					read-only key, so a dashboard or a support script can check without claiming.
				</p>
			</section>

			<section class="band">
				<h2>Using it with the others</h2>
				<p>
					<a href="/relay/">Relay</a> delivers at-least-once and every call carries
					<code>X-NanoRelay-Run-Id</code>, which is exactly the kind of key this takes. Pass it
					here and a retried run stops being a second run.
				</p>
				<p class="cta">
					<a class="button" href="/howto/">How to, in curl</a>
					<a class="button secondary" href="/about/">What this is</a>
				</p>
			</section>
		</main>
		<Footer />
	</body>
</html>

<style>
	.hero {
		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns: 1.05fr 1fr;
		gap: 2.5em;
		align-items: center;
		padding: 2em 0 1em;
	}
	.hero > * {
		min-width: 0;
	}
	.dim {
		color: var(--muted);
	}
	.lede {
		font-size: 1.1rem;
		color: var(--muted);
		max-width: 34em;
	}
	.cta {
		display: flex;
		gap: 0.7em;
		flex-wrap: wrap;
		margin: 1.4em 0 0.6em;
	}
	.fineprint {
		font-size: 0.85rem;
	}
	.band {
		padding: 3em 0 0;
	}
	.band > p {
		max-width: 46em;
		color: var(--muted);
	}
	.small {
		font-size: 0.85rem;
	}
	.two {
		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
		gap: 1.2em;
		margin-top: 1.4em;
	}
	.alive-edge {
		border-color: var(--alive-dim);
	}
	ul.plain {
		list-style: none;
		margin: 1em 0 0;
		padding: 0;
		font-size: 0.95rem;
	}
	ul.plain li {
		padding-left: 1.3em;
		position: relative;
		margin-bottom: 0.6em;
		color: var(--muted);
	}
	ul.plain li::before {
		content: "·";
		position: absolute;
		left: 0.3em;
	}
	.alive-edge ul.plain li::before {
		content: "✓";
		color: var(--alive);
		left: 0;
	}
	@media (max-width: 860px) {
		.hero,
		.two {
			grid-template-columns: 1fr;
		}
	}
</style>
